ef 1075 #edb/ref 17592186530116 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530387 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530387 #edb/ref 1077 "field-2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530387 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186530116 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530387 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "e6785073-50ec-528b-8583-c2326c8a4528" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530392 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186530124 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530392 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530392 #edb/ref 1077 "field-1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530392 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186530124 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530392 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "ee1f9f9d-34b1-5294-af05-80a2cd312768" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530433 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186530228 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530433 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186530392 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530433 #edb/ref 1069 "q-342097" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530433 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530433 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/78/781d755a292a18934af5b28436593262070527aa41da3bda092f979d388ef1f7.png)\n\nIf the figure above shows the graph of $f(x),$ then\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\,-{2}^{-}}f(x)=$  \n{{field-1}},\n$\\,{lim}_{x\\to \\,-{2}^{+}}f(x)=$  \n{{field-2}}." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530433 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "2100ce73-4770-5a89-a8b5-3f4b9726facd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530433 #edb/ref 1074 "As $x$ approaches $-2$ from the *left*, the function $f(x)$ approaches $0.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,-{2}^{-}}f(x)=0.\n$$\n\nAs $x$ approaches $-2$ from the *right*, the function $f(x)$ approaches $2.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,-{2}^{+}}f(x)=2.\n$$\n\nIn conclusion, we have\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,-{2}^{-}}f(x)=0,\\,{lim}_{x\\to \\,-{2}^{+}}f(x)=2.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530442 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186530349 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530442 #edb/ref 1069 "q-106457" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530442 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530442 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/21/21cf8b9a6a94900b68a565a2d0915ba849379a134009c37db64bd5ff1eea2ac7.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true concerning the function $y=f(x)$ whose graph is shown above? \n\nI. ${lim}_{x\\to \\,-{2}^{-}}f(x)={lim}_{x\\to \\,-{2}^{+}}f(x)$\n\nII. ${lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{+}}f(x)={lim}_{x\\to \\,{2}^{-}}f(x)$\n\nIII. ${lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{-}}f(x)={lim}_{x\\to \\,{2}^{+}}f(x)$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530442 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "2e89b2a9-3a87-5f49-9315-671676fe1a51" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530442 #edb/ref 1074 "First, let's compute the limits in question. \n\nAs $x$ approaches $-2$ from the left, the function value $f(x)$ approaches $y=2,$ and consequently\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,-{2}^{-}}f(x)=2\\,.\n$$\n\nAs $x$ approaches $-2$ from the right, the function value $f(x)$ approaches $y=-2,$ and consequently\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,-{2}^{+}}f(x)=-2\\,.\n$$\n\nWe cannot approach $x=1$ from the right because the function is not defined to the right of $x=1.$ Consequently,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{+}}f(x)=\\text{DNE}\\,.\n$$\n\nSimilarly, we cannot approach $2$ from the left because the function is not defined to the left of $x=2.$ Consequently,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,{2}^{-}}f(x)=\\text{DNE}\\,.\n$$\n\nAs $x$ approaches $1$ from the left, the function value $f(x)$ approaches $y=1,$ and consequently\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{-}}f(x)=1\\,.\n$$\n\nAs $x$ approaches $2$ from the right, the function value $f(x)$ approaches $y=1,$ and consequently\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,{2}^{+}}f(x)=1\\,.\n$$\n\nNow, let's look at each statement in turn.\n\n- I. ${lim}_{x\\to \\,-{2}^{-}}f(x)={lim}_{x\\to \\,-{2}^{+}}f(x)$ becomes $2=-2,$ which is false.\n\n- II. ${lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{+}}f(x)={lim}_{x\\to \\,{2}^{-}}f(x)$ is false because neither one-sided limit exists.\n\n- III. ${lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{-}}f(x)={lim}_{x\\to \\,{2}^{+}}f(x)$ becomes $1=1,$ which is true.\n\nThus, only statement III is true." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530458 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186530240 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530458 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186530321 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530458 #edb/ref 1069 "q-342118" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530458 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530458 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/44/441d472f60a9cc7415799032f66d2a618bf8419336f52aefb47f3b1ae7640875.png)\n\nIf the figure above shows the graph of $f(x),$ then\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\,{3}^{-}}f(x)=$\n{{field-1}}$,$\n$\\,{lim}_{x\\to \\,{3}^{+}}f(x)=$\n{{field-2}}." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530458 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "585630ea-edc2-5584-9703-3115d4a68d23" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530458 #edb/ref 1074 "As $x$ approaches $3$ from the *left*, the function $f(x)$ approaches $2.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,{3}^{-}}f(x)=2.\n$$\n\nAs $x$ approaches $3$ from the *right*, the function $f(x)$ approaches $2.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,{3}^{+}}f(x)=2.\n$$\n\nIn conclusion, we have\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,{3}^{-}}f(x)={lim}_{x\\to \\,{3}^{+}}f(x)=2.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530522 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186530371 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530522 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186530387 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530522 #edb/ref 1069 "q-342137" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530522 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530522 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/bd/bd7ee98596021320d0f24d5b813de476ffabaa48156316091e5f23fed9e20d30.png)\n\nThe function $y=g(x)$ (shown above) is defined on $x\\in (-\\infty ,-1)\\cup (2,\\infty ).$ Find the left-sided limit at $x=-1$ and the right-sided limit at $x=2.$\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\,-{1}^{-}}g(x)=$\n{{field-1}}$,$\n$\\,{lim}_{x\\to \\,{2}^{+}}g(x)=$\n{{field-2}}." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530522 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "c6b36716-81d8-575e-98da-f48320938f53" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530522 #edb/ref 1074 "As $x$ approaches $-1$ from the left, the function $g(x)$ approaches $1.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,-{1}^{-}}g(x)=1.\n$$\n\nSimilarly, as $x$ approaches $2$ from the right, the function $g(x)$ approaches $0.$ Consequently,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,{2}^{+}}g(x)=0.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530531 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186530235 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530531 #edb/ref 1069 "q-106434" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530531 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530531 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/f0/f01058e0460ac0de134f36ce8a83aada0a719565f78c640b713526ec1b1a3a99.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true concerning the function $y=f(x)$ whose graph is shown above?  \n\nI. ${lim}_{x\\to \\,-{3}^{+}}f(x)=0$\n\nII. ${lim}_{x\\to \\,-{1}^{+}}f(x)=\\text{DNE}$\n\nIII. ${lim}_{x\\to \\,-{1}^{-}}f(x)={lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{+}}f(x)$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530531 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ddfb9440-a491-530c-8883-b3b8364d490f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530531 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's look at each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. As $x$ approaches $-3$ from the right, the function value $f(x)$ approaches $y=0,$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,-{3}^{+}}f(x)=0\\,.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. As $x$ approaches $-1$ from the right, the function value $f(x)$ is undefined, Therefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,-{1}^{+}}f(x)=\\text{DNE}\\,.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. As $x$ approaches $-1$ from the left, the function value $f(x)$ approaches $y=-2$, and consequently\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,-{1}^{-}}f(x)=-2\\,.\n$$\n\nSimilarly, as $x$ approaches $1$ from the right, the function value $f(x)$ approaches $y=0$, and consequently\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{+}}f(x)=0\\,.\n$$\n\n Therefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,-{1}^{-}}f(x)\\ne {lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{+}}f(x)\\,.\n$$\n\nTherefore, only statements I and II are true." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530542 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186530286 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530542 #edb/ref 1069 "q-106429" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530542 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530542 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/52/52478c6cdcae0ba1e46b34eea8a4115f2f5f14d8973604c5ab589842bb7db262.png)\n\nThe function $y=f(x)$ (shown above) is defined on $x\\in [-4,1).$ Find the right-sided limit at $x=-4$ and the left-sided limit at $x=1.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530542 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "f85c0d08-ef45-5aca-a806-01e365550a2b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530542 #edb/ref 1074 "As $x$ approaches $-4$ from the *right*, the function $f(x)$ approaches $-1.$ Consequently,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,-{4}^{+}}f(x)=-1\\,.\n$$\n\nLikewise, as $x$ approaches $1$ from the *left*, the function $f(x)$ approaches $3.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{-}}f(x)=3\\,.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530547 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186530224 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530547 #edb/ref 1069 "q-34846" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530547 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530547 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/11/11e1c9a550944fc8435fc0d6f17be7d172e0c6d8f82fc1af950896f6ec8176b6.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true concerning the function $y=f(x)$ whose graph is shown above?  \n\nI. ${lim}_{x\\to \\,{0}^{+}}f(x)=\\text{DNE}$\n\nII. ${lim}_{x\\to \\,{2}^{-}}f(x)=1$\n\nIII. ${lim}_{x\\to \\,-{1}^{+}}f(x)={lim}_{x\\to \\,-{1}^{-}}f(x)$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530547 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ffd5c800-ccb9-56a9-9fa0-86118664e9d6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530547 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's look at each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. As $x$ approaches $0$ from the right, the function value $f(x)$  approaches $y=1.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,{0}^{+}}f(x)=1.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. As $x$ approaches $2$ from the left, the function value $f(x)$ approaches $y=1$, Therefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,{2}^{-}}f(x)=1.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. As $x$ approaches $-1$ from the right, the function value $f(x)$ is undefined. Consequently,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,-{1}^{+}}f(x)=\\text{DNE}.\n$$\n\nInstead, as $x$ approaches $-1$ from the left, the function value $f(x)$ approaches $y=1$, and consequently\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,-{1}^{-}}f(x)=1.\n$$\n\n Therefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,-{1}^{-}}f(x)\\ne {lim}_{x\\to \\,-{1}^{+}}f(x).\n$$\n\nTherefore, only statement II is true." #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
